course: dictionary
lesson: 13880
title: Informal words and slang
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to casual talk with friends and family. Knackered is one of them, and you will hear it a lot in Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. «After the football match, Sam flopped on the sofa: "I'm absolutely knackered."» The words around knackered tell you Sam is very tired after playing.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. «The old bike has a flat tyre and a broken chain. It's knackered.» Here the bike is not tired at all. It is broken, or too worn out to work properly. Always read the clues around the word to find which meaning fits.
explain: Knackered is informal, so it suits chatting but not school essays or formal letters. In exam writing, use a plainer word such as exhausted or broken. A good check is to ask yourself, "Would I say this to my head teacher?"
fact: Knackered is slang, used in casual speech in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «Knackered» means extremely tired or worn out, like «exhausted».
fact: «Knackered» can also mean broken, or too old and worn to work properly.
fact: Phrases you may hear are «absolutely knackered», «completely knackered» and «I'm knackered».
fact: Slang is fine in a story with real dialogue, but it is too informal for exam writing.
exercise: «After the long hike, we were all knackered.» What does knackered mean here? | *Extremely tired | Lost in the woods | Very hungry | Full of energy and ready to run
exercise: «The old washing machine is knackered and keeps breaking down.» What does knackered suggest? | *Broken, too worn to work properly | Brand new and shiny | Loud and fast | Very clean
exercise: Ella says, «I'm knackered», before her exam. Is knackered a good word to use in an exam essay? | Yes, but only in maths | No, it means happy | Yes, it is always the best word | *No, it is informal slang, so write exhausted instead
test: «Dad said, "I'm completely knackered after work."» What does completely knackered mean? | Wide awake and cheerful | Slightly cold | *Totally exhausted | Lost in the dark
test: Which sentence uses knackered to mean broken? | I'm knackered, so I'm going to bed | Dad is absolutely knackered after the run | We are knackered after the long walk | *The old car is knackered, it won't start
exam: Which word would be best in a formal exam essay instead of knackered? | Wee | *Exhausted | Knackered | Mid
